{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c623","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a079c9-a389-7b22-a682-8d880628647c"},"sequence":364,"recorded_at":1780531400000723,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"15fbab40-2656-4b01-9f2b-97bf36f3de82","payload_type":"task.lifecycle.output","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"task_lifecycle","command_id":"15fbab40-2656-4b01-9f2b-97bf36f3de82","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"15fbab40-2656-4b01-9f2b-97bf36f3de82"},"task_stream":{"kind":"task","id":"9e4fccb7-2dea-4565-8875-7f92e8a0120a"},"task_id":"9e4fccb7-2dea-4565-8875-7f92e8a0120a","event":{"kind":"output","task_id":"9e4fccb7-2dea-4565-8875-7f92e8a0120a","chunk":"Read text file `/Users/iv/.agents/skills/project-management/references/tracked-background-spawn.md`.\n(279 line(s) above)\n280|| `code`, `docs`, `research` producers | Yes |\n281|| Reviewer of such a leaf | Yes — the same workspace, holding the same lease so the candidate cannot drift during review |\n282|| Element with no containing Story | No — there is no Story-scoped workspace to provision |\n283|\n284|The first three rows are decided facts about what the run *is*, so they produce\n285|no workspace and the spawn proceeds in the control root. A repository question is\n286|not one of them. When a run in the \"Yes\" rows has no repository to isolate in,\n287|the spawn **refuses** rather than quietly falling back to the shared checkout:\n288|\n289|| Control root | Result |\n290||---|---|\n291|| Not a Git working tree | `worktree_not_a_git_repository`, before assignment, prompt, RUN, or child |\n292|| Git state unreadable (git missing, probe failed) | `worktree_git_command_failed` — unknown, not absent |\n293|\n294|Both refusals are evaluated after the §9.1 local-board capability precondition,\n295|so a resolved remote board still reports `worktree_isolation_requires_local_board`\n296|first and an operator is never sent to fix a repository that was never the\n297|blocker.\n298|\n299|### Scheduling\n300|\n301|A run waiting for a Story lease stays `queued` and **consumes no\n302|`spawn.max_parallel` slot**, and it does not block independent Stories queued\n303|behind it. Admission takes Story eligibility first and a global slot second, and\n304|releases both on every terminal path.\n305|\n306|### Story lifecycle boundaries\n307|\n308|**Do not attach new repository work to an integrated Story.** Its managed\n309|workspace is spent and its base is frozen at the pre-integration trunk, so a\n310|new producer would build from stale authority and replay work that already\n311|landed. This happened when 15 of 22 paths in a post-integration candidate\n312|duplicated paths already on trunk. Create a new Story and workspace from current\n313|trunk for the new work.\n314|\n315|**Do not land a trunk change while a Story is in flight.** Every in-flight Story\n316|then needs a base refresh before its tree can land, multiplying revalidation and\n317|conflict risk across the whole wave. In the observed failure, trunk and the\n318|Story had independently fixed the same defect on the same config line, so the\n319|required replay aborted with a conflict. Sequence trunk landing around active\n320|Stories rather than forcing every Story to absorb the moving base.\n321|\n322|### Change Requests, review, rework, and checkpoint\n323|\n324|A producer that hands off inside a managed workspace publishes a **Change\n325|Request**: a revisioned record attached to the existing Task/Bug/Story, not a new\n326|board element. It carries the branch, the base and candidate tree OIDs, a binary\n327|patch resource with its SHA-256, the changed-path list, and an explicit\n328|`repository_delta` of `present` or `empty`.\n329|\n330|The candidate is snapshotted through a temporary alternate Git index, so tracked\n331|changes, deletions, and untracked non-ignored files are all captured **without\n332|touching the managed worktree's own index**. Every `.task-board/**` entry is then\n333|restored from the base tree, which is why a `changed_paths` list can never\n334|contain a board path — the authoritative board is the control root, and a\n335|worktree's `.task-board` copy is a checkout artifact. Paths dropped by that rule\n336|are recorded as `dropped_board_paths` and shown by `worktree status` and in the\n337|reviewer's prompt. A non-empty list is a diagnostic, usually a `git add -A` in\n338|the child, not a failure.\n339|\n340|Construction runs for **every** producer completion in a managed workspace,\n341|whether or not the run carries a delivery goal. Managed workspace activation\n342|turns on role, task class, containing Story and local board alone, so an ordinary\n343|`task-board spawn TASK-... --role developer --background` gets an isolated\n344|worktree — and the half of the feature that makes that worktree reviewable\n345|activates on exactly the same conditions.\n346|\n347|It runs **after** the spawn completion artifact guard, and on the same two facts:\n348|the board reached the run's end status, and the run attached a new or updated\n349|task-scoped outcome artifact. A run that attached nothing is reverted to its\n350|producer status by that guard and leaves no Change Request at all. A read that\n351|FAILS is reported as a failure and never as \"did not hand off\".\n352|\n353|A workspace-claiming producer that publishes nothing says why, on the run's event\n354|log and in a board note: `handoff_unsatisfied`, with the detail — the board is\n355|not at the end status, or no task-scoped outcome artifact was attached.\n356|\n357|The other two reasons are recorded nowhere, on one rule: a reason that is true of\n358|every run of its class says nothing about the run in front of you, and repeating\n359|it on every completion buries the case the reporting exists for. An un-isolated\n360|run skips with `no_managed_workspace`, which is true of every un-isolated run. A\n361|reviewer-archetype run skips with `not_a_producer`, which is true of every\n362|reviewer run — a reviewer judges a candidate rather than publishing one, so\n363|noting it would append a line describing correct behaviour to the element's\n364|progress on every single review cycle.\n365|\n366|`repository_delta=empty` means the producer changed no repository file. It is an\n367|attribute, never a state and never terminal: the Change Request is `ready` like\n368|any other, a reviewer **is** spawned, and the injected prompt says in plain terms\n369|that nothing changed and lists the outcome resources that were produced. Deciding\n370|whether that was correct is a judgement about intent versus delivery, which is\n371|what the reviewer archetype is for.\n372|\n373|**The kind is derived from the board, never declared.** A Change Request is\n374|`task_delta` or `story_final`, and the second is what `worktree integrate`\n375|accepts as the Story's integration unit. Publication asks the board whether\n376|completing this leaf would also close its Story — the same question\n377|`worktree checkpoint` asks to REFUSE that leaf, through the same call — so the\n378|leaf one command declines is exactly the leaf the other treats as the Story's\n379|unit. Every indeterminate answer refuses the publication: no board access, an\n380|unreadable board, or an element the board does not contain means the question was\n381|never answered, and answering `task_delta` on a failed read would leave the Story\n382|permanently unintegrable with nothing recording why.\n383|\n384|**The base follows the kind.** A `task_delta` is measured from the Story branch\n385|tip, where earlier leaves already checkpointed. A `story_final` is measured from\n386|the workspace record's **current base OID**, initially the exact selected base\n387|OID produced by upstream freshness resolution and later advanced only by a\n388|successful final-leaf refresh or by final publication after it proves selected\n389|upstream authority, checkpoint, managed branch, and checked-out HEAD have one\n390|consistent provenance. Publication accepts either the unchanged checkpoint with\n391|an uncommitted candidate in the worktree, or a branch/HEAD exactly one direct,\n392|single-parent commit past that checkpoint when the commit tree equals the tree\n393|independently snapshotted from the selected base and present worktree. It is\n394|never recomputed through the local trunk ref: that ref may remain behind the\n395|upstream that provisioning fetched, which would expand the patch to\n396|already-merged predecessor work. Any OID/tree/provenance mismatch refuses before\n397|validation or partial Change Request persistence. The candidate tree is a full\n398|snapshot, so the recorded base is also the stable input to later re-parenting\n399|when trunk moves again.\n400|\n401|**A Story has at most one unaccepted producer candidate.** The worktree is\n402|Story-scoped and the snapshotter has no durable per-task path ownership model.\n403|Before a producer starts, spawn therefore scans the Story's Change Requests and\n404|refuses `change_request_sibling_producer_blocked` when another element owns a\n405|`draft`, `ready`, `reviewing`, `changes_requested`, `stale`, or `conflicted`\n406|revision. The refusal names the blocking task and is checked both before\n407|provisioning and after lease admission to close the concurrent-spawn race.\n408|Reviewers bypass the producer gate, and the same element may start rework for\n409|its own revision.\n410|\n411|**The producer runs the landing suite and records tree-bound evidence.** When\n412|`spawn.worktree_isolation.validation.commands` is configured, publication runs it\n413|in the Story worktree and records `validation` keyed to the published candidate\n414|tree. The suite runs against the WORKTREE while the evidence claims a TREE, so\n415|the candidate is recomputed afterwards and compared; a suite that moved the\n416|worktree — a build that drops artifacts, a codegen step, a formatter — yields\n417|**no** evidence rather than evidence keyed to a tree it never saw. A failing\n418|suite is fail-sticky at its first non-zero command and cannot publish a `ready`\n419|candidate: the immutable revision is immediately `changes_requested`, its\n420|bounded transcript is attached as a task-scoped system resource, and validation\n421|records the one-based failed command index/total plus the real exit status. The\n422|producer run follows the existing non-success/recovery-successor path with a\n423|retry step naming that resource. A command that could not run at all is never\n424|recorded as a pass. Passing and explicitly empty configured suites retain the\n425|ready/success path. See\n426|[how the evidence binds](#validation-binds-to-the-tree-that-lands).\n427|\n428|**Write validation commands for the managed worktree they actually run in.**\n429|The validation suite runs inside that worktree alongside gitignored scratch and\n430|reviewer probes, so repository-wide filesystem walks and index comparisons can\n431|inspect the wrong population. One project hit all three failures in the same\n432|suite:\n433|\n434|| Gate | Naive command | Observed failure |\n435|| --- | --- | --- |\n436|| Generated-file freshness | `go generate && git diff --exit-code -- <file>` | `git diff` compared the producer's intentionally unstaged file against the index and refused a correct candidate tree. |\n437|| JSON validity | `find . -name '*.json' \\| xargs ...` | `find` walked 287 files including scratch, then `xargs` failed with `command line cannot be assembled, too long`. |\n438|| Formatting | `test -z \"$(gofmt -l .)\"` | `gofmt` descended into `.temp/` and reported a reviewer's probe as a product formatting failure. |\n439|\n(more lines below)"}}}
